<section class="faq">
  <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
  <details>
    <summary>What is Schema Markup and why do I need it?</summary>
    <p>Schema markup is structured data code (JSON-LD) that you add to your website to help search engines understand your content better. It enables rich results in Google like star ratings, FAQ accordions, event dates, and more — which can significantly improve your click-through rate.</p>
  </details>
  <details>
    <summary>How do I add the generated JSON-LD to my website?</summary>
    <p>Copy the generated code and paste it inside a <code>&lt;script type="application/ld+json"&gt;</code> tag in your page's <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> or before the closing <code>&lt;/body&gt;</code> tag. You can also add it via your CMS's custom HTML section.</p>
  </details>
  <details>
    <summary>How can I test if my Schema Markup is valid?</summary>
    <p>Use Google's <a href="https://schema.org/tools/validator" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Structured Data Testing Tool</a> or the <a href="https://search.google.com/test/rich-results" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rich Results Test</a>.</p>
  </details>
  <details>
    <summary>Does Schema Markup improve SEO rankings?</summary>
    <p>Schema markup doesn't directly affect rankings, but it enables rich results (stars, FAQs, breadcrumbs) that increase visibility and click-through rates. Higher CTR can indirectly improve rankings over time.</p>
  </details>
  <details>
    <summary>Can I use multiple schema types on one page?</summary>
    <p>Yes! You can include multiple JSON-LD script blocks on the same page. For example, an article page might use Article schema for the content, Organization schema for the publisher, and BreadcrumbList for navigation.</p>
  </details>
</section>
